## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to optimize prompts for cost and speed, exercising token budgeting and simple NLP heuristics in prompt engineering.

## Key Concepts
- Token counting and budgets
- Intent preservation
- Heuristic trimming

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal compress
function compress(p){
  // naive: drop time qualifiers and rephrase
  let q = p
    .replace(/in the last \d+ months?/i, "")
    .replace(/and export\s+their\s+emails\s+to\s+a\s+CSV\s+file/gi, "export emails to CSV");
  return q.trim();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate compression ratio vs fidelity?
- How would you handle languages other than English?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime safety gates for prompt orchestration in a production analytics environment with compliance constraints and auditable decisions.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy store (JSON) for dynamic gating
- Risk scoring based on injection patterns and data sensitivity
- Module routing: SQL-QueryGenerator, DataMasker, OutputFormatter
- Input sanitization and audit trails
- Test harness for injections and safe prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(input, policy) {
  const risk = policy.score(input);
  if (risk > policy.maxAllowed) return {blocked: true};
  // pick module based on intent
  const module = policy.chooseModule(input);
  return {module, plan: 'execute', payload: {input}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version and roll back policies in production?
- How would you simulate data drift affecting risk scores?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect provenance-aware prompt pipelines at scale, ensuring data lineage, watermarking, and auditability across tenants with realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and lineage in LLM prompts
- Per-tenant guards, watermarking, policy enforcement
- Output verification with source citations via a mock knowledge graph
- Structured audit logging: input hash, rewritten prompt, per-model outputs, latency, drift verdict
- Security considerations around prompt leakage and PII

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function rewriteWithProvenance(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  const provenance = `Source=${tenantPolicy.allowedSources.join('|')};Tenant=${tenantPolicy.id}`;
  return `${prompt}\n\n[Provenance:${provenance}]`;
}
function verifyOutput(output, sources) {
  const hasCitation = sources.some(s => output.includes(s));
  return hasCitation;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle non-determinism across models while preserving provenance guarantees?
- How would you extend the audit log to support compliance reviews and exception handling?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design modular prompt orchestration and a concrete lightweight prototype. Checks how candidates formalize routing signals and edge-case handling in real-world content workflows.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt architecture and routing signals
- Edge-case handling for content-critical prompts
- Lightweight Python prototype for quick validation
- Payload shaping for downstream components

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (JavaScript-like pseudocode)
function routePrompt(query) {
  const q = (query || '').toLowerCase();
  const module = (q.includes('source') || q.includes('earnings') || q.includes('did'))
    ? 'Source-Validator' 
    : q.includes('hallucination') ? 'Hallucination-Detector' : 'Answer-Formatter';
  return {
    module,
    plan: ['collect sources','assess credibility'],
    payload: { query, required_sources: 5, confidence_target: 'high' }
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the routing decisions across diverse prompts?
- What metrics would you track to improve module selection over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant policy enforcement, guard-detection, and prompt rewriting with auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant policy registry and per-tenant constraints
- Guard-detection vs. prompt rewriting
- System guard injection and explicit privacy constraints
- Audit artifacts and drift/telemetry considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const policy = {
  Tesla: { pii: true, retentionDays: 90 },
  Google: { pii: false, retentionDays: 365 },
  Plaid: { pii: true, retentionDays: 30 }
};
function guardAndRewrite(tenant, prompt) {
  const violations = [];
  const lower = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (lower.includes("password") || lower.includes("ssn")) {
    violations.push("PII-like content");
  }
  const rewritten = `[${tenant}] ${prompt} /* guard: ensure policy compliance */`;
  return { rewritten, violations };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate determinism and drift for two tenants under evolving policies?  
- What tests would you add to catch policy-evading prompts without compromising latency?
